  "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{\u2033}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+64{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+64) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+64=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-64 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 8\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda =\\pm 8\\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation. \n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{8\\text{i}t})=\\operatorname{cos}\u20618t$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{8\\text{i}t})=\\operatorname{sin}\u20618t$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\operatorname{sin}\u20618t-2\\operatorname{cos}\u20614t$ is not a solution.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
